WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.452
Coding Exercise 5 combines filtering, calculation, and classification.

00:00:05.452 --> 00:00:08.997
First, keep only completed admissions.

00:00:08.997 --> 00:00:14.792
A completed admission has a discharge date, so use an explicit missing-value test.

00:00:14.792 --> 00:00:21.007
Next, calculate length of stay by subtracting the admission date from the discharge date.

00:00:21.007 --> 00:00:23.797
Then classify the result. Fewer than five days is Short.

00:00:23.797 --> 00:00:26.287
Five through ten days is Medium.

00:00:26.287 --> 00:00:28.702
More than ten days is Long.

00:00:28.702 --> 00:00:31.062
Pay close attention to boundaries.

00:00:31.062 --> 00:00:36.362
Exactly five days should be Medium.

00:00:36.362 --> 00:00:39.427
Exactly ten days should also be Medium.

00:00:39.427 --> 00:00:46.062
If an ongoing admission appears in your output, review the WHERE clause before changing the CASE expression.

00:00:46.062 --> 00:00:50.852
The first issue would be population selection, not classification.

00:00:50.852 --> 00:00:58.587
This exercise reinforces a useful workflow: define the valid population first, calculate the measure second,

00:00:58.587 --> 00:01:00.061
and classify it third.
